Font weight and style on Linux when the family has no face of them. GPUI's cosmic-text system matched a request to
the family's closest face and then drew that face as it is: a variable face (one face for every weight, as GNOME's
Adwaita Sans and Cantarell ship) at its default instance, shaped at the face's own weight and rasterized without
variation coordinates, so every bold and semibold text came out regular; a family with no bold or no italic face drew
its regular one. Now `font_id` hands out, next to the matched face, a font of its own for each way it must be drawn
otherwise (`Synthesis`, kept per face in `styled_font_ids`):
- a variable face whose `wght` axis holds the requested weight is loaded at that weight (cosmic-text's `get_font`),
  shaped at it (the run's attributes carry it, so cosmic-text picks the same face at that instance) and rasterized at
  it (the swash scaler's `wght` variation);
- otherwise a face lighter than 600 asked for at 600 or more is emboldened by a 24th of the em, as FreeType's
  `FT_GlyphSlot_Embolden`; an upright face asked for italic or oblique is slanted 14 degrees. Advances stay the face's.
Emoji fonts are never synthesized. A face drawn as it is keeps its `FontId`.
scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to the verified crates.io archive after 0001.

